course: dictionary
lesson: 7950
title: Rebuild and the prefix re-
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The prefix re- means again. When you add re- to a word, you get the action done once more. Build means to make something, so rebuild means to make it again. You can see the same pattern in «rebuilt» in «its body is rebuilt into a butterfly».
explain: Rebuild has two uses. The first is about real things: after a flood, fire or war, a town is rebuilt. «Most of the Great Wall you see in photos today was built or rebuilt by the Ming dynasty.» The second is about things you cannot touch, like trust, strength or a team. «A proper sorry can slowly rebuild trust.»
explain: Look for clue words. Slow and quick tell you that rebuilding can take longer than breaking. «Trust is quick to lose and slow to rebuild.» To check your meaning, put «make again» in place of rebuild. If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Re- is a prefix, a small part added to the front of a word that changes its meaning.
fact: «Rebuild» means to build something again after it has been damaged or destroyed.
fact: «Rebuild» can also mean to make something strong again, like your strength, trust or a team.
fact: «Rebuilt» is the past form: «The Ming rebuilt and greatly extended walls in brick and stone.»
fact: Other re- words, such as «reread», «rewrite» and «restart», also mean doing the action again.
exercise: «A flood damaged the bridge, so the town will rebuild it.» What does rebuild mean here? | To knock it down for ever | *To build it again after it was damaged | To paint it bright red | To visit it on holiday
exercise: In the word rebuild, what does the prefix re- mean? | Very big | Not | *Again | Under
exercise: «Trust is quick to lose and slow to rebuild.» Find and copy one word that shows rebuilding takes longer than losing. | lose | *slow | quick | trust
test: Which sentence uses rebuild to mean make something strong again, not build a real thing? | The Ming rebuilt the walls in brick. | The workers will rebuild the old bridge. | *A proper sorry can slowly rebuild trust. | Dad will rebuild the garden shed.
test: Which word is the past form of rebuild, as in «its body is rebuilt into a butterfly»? | rebuilding | rebuild | rebuilds | *rebuilt
exam: Ben says rebuild means to break something again. Is he right? | Yes, because re- means to break | *No, re- means again, so rebuild means to build something again | Only if the thing was made of brick | Yes, because the word has build in it, so it must mean break
